    "slug": "us-signs-military-base-deal-with-puntland-bypassing-mogadishu",
    "summary": "The United States has signed a new security agreement with Puntland, Somalia's northeastern federal member state, during talks in Bosaso. The deal, which bypasses the federal government in Mogadishu, reflects Washington's growing frustration with political infighting in the capital and its impact on counterterrorism efforts.",
    "content": "The United States has formalized a new military agreement with Puntland, a semi-autonomous region in northeastern Somalia, following discussions held on Sunday in the port city of Bosaso. The deal establishes a US military presence in the area, marking a significant shift in Washington's engagement with Somalia's federal structure.\n\nOfficials in Puntland confirmed the agreement, which was signed without the involvement of the federal government in Mogadishu. This move underscores a broader US strategy of engaging directly with regional administrations, bypassing the central government amid ongoing political paralysis in the capital.\n\nThe agreement comes against the backdrop of a prolonged political crisis in Mogadishu, where disputes over electoral processes and power-sharing have stalled governance and distracted from national security priorities. US officials have expressed frustration with the federal government's inability to unify against militant groups like Al-Shabaab and Islamic State in Somalia (ISIS-Somalia).\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this deal has direct implications. Puntland is home to major diaspora investments and is a key transit point for remittances that sustain many families. A stronger US security presence could stabilize the region, potentially boosting economic confidence and safe travel for diaspora members visiting or investing in their homeland. However, it also raises questions about the fragmentation of Somalia's central authority, which could complicate diplomatic and security coordination.\n\nThe agreement signals a hardening of the US 'dual-track' approach, which has been evolving for years. By partnering directly with Puntland, Washington aims to maintain pressure on militant groups while sidestepping Mogadishu's dysfunction. Analysts caution that this could further weaken the federal government's legitimacy and deepen regional divides.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-us-puntland-bosaso-military-base-deal-somaliland/",
