    "summary": "Iran's foreign ministry spokesperson stated that Tehran is not in negotiations with Washington, dismissing reports of renewed talks. Current discussions are limited to bilateral talks with Oman focusing on the Strait of Hormuz.",
    "content": "Iran has officially denied that it is currently engaged in any negotiations with the United States, pushing back against recent speculation that the two nations were restarting dialogue. Esmaeil Baghaei, spokesperson for Iran's Ministry of Foreign Affairs, clarified on Monday that the only ongoing talks are those with Oman, and these discussions are strictly bilateral in nature.\n\nThe remarks come in response to media reports suggesting a possible thaw in Iran-U.S. relations, which have been strained for years over issues including Tehran's nuclear program and regional activities. Baghaei emphasized that the dialogue with Oman, which centers on strategic matters related to the Strait of Hormuz, has no connection to Washington. This vital waterway is a critical passage for global oil shipments, and any developments there carry international significance.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom have family and business ties in the Gulf region, understanding Iranian diplomatic moves is important. The stability of the Strait of Hormuz directly affects global energy prices and maritime security, which in turn impacts remittances and trade for Somalia. Additionally, Iran's regional role often influences broader geopolitical dynamics that affect the Horn of Africa.\n\nThe denial underscores the complexities of Middle Eastern diplomacy, where even indirect channels can be misinterpreted. As Iran maintains its stance, observers will watch whether any future indirect engagements emerge, but for now, Tehran's position is clear: no talks with the U.S., only with Oman.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/03/iiraan-oo-sheegtay-inay-wada-hadal-kula-jirin-mareykanka/",
