    "original_title": "Somalia Pushes Trade Reform as EAC and AfCFTA Membership Expose Customs Challenges",
    "slug": "somalia-ramps-up-customs-reforms-to-leverage-regional-trade-deals",
    "summary": "Somalia's National Trade Facilitation Committee warns that without accelerating customs and border reforms, the country cannot fully benefit from EAC and AfCFTA membership, urging faster regulatory alignment to unlock regional trade opportunities.",
    "content": "Somalia's trade facilitation body, the National Trade Facilitation Committee, has underscored the need for accelerated reforms in customs and border procedures to capitalize on the country's membership in the East African Community (EAC) and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA). The committee's latest assessment indicates that while these memberships present significant economic opportunities, the country's current regulatory framework poses a barrier to realizing them.\n\nThe committee's call comes amid broader efforts to integrate Somalia into regional and continental markets. Since joining the EAC in 2023 and ratifying the AfCFTA, Somalia has been working to align its trade policies with regional standards. However, the pace of change has been slow, with customs operations and border management still lagging behind the requirements of these agreements.\n\nKey challenges include outdated customs laws, inefficient border checkpoints, and a lack of coordination among various government agencies involved in trade. The committee emphasizes that without streamlining these processes, Somali businesses will struggle to compete with counterparts in other member states, hindering potential growth in exports and cross-border trade.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom are involved in trade and investment between Somalia and other countries, these reforms are particularly pertinent. Improved customs procedures could mean faster clearance times, lower costs, and greater predictability for traders, encouraging more diaspora investment in the country's economy. Moreover, successful integration into regional markets could open up new opportunities for Somali goods, from livestock to agricultural products, which have historically been vital sectors.\n\nMoving forward, the committee plans to work with international partners and regional bodies to modernize infrastructure, train customs officials, and harmonize regulations. While progress may be gradual, the long-term benefits of a reformed trade environment are clear: a more robust economy, stronger regional ties, and a better business climate for both local and diaspora entrepreneurs.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/somalia-pushes-trade-reform-as-eac-and-afcfta-membership-expose-customs-challenges/424432/",
